10175: 6.4 I/O Controller Registers
10176: 
10177:             The IOC implements a number of registers to control its operation and
10178:             facilitate its testing. The CPU can access the IOC registers directly, but they
10179:             are not accessible from the PCI bus.
10180:             All references to the IOC registers must allow for quadword access; longword
10181:             access is not supported.
10182:             The IOC registers are described in ascending-address order in Sections 6.4.1
10183:             through 6.4.14. Unless stated otherwise, the assertion of reset_in_l does not
10184:             modify any IOC register bits and all IOC register bits are UNDEFINED after
10185:             a power-up reset.
10186: 
10187:                                                                  Note
10188:                   In the register field description tables, the abbreviations in the Type
10189:                   column indicate field access behavior. The abbreviations are defined in
10190:                   the Conventions section of the Preface.
10191: 
10192:                                                                                                                I/O Controller 6-37
10193: 6.4.1 Host Address Extension Register
10194: 
10195:             In the sparse memory space, the lower bits of the CPU address indicate
10196:             transfer size, and the address is shifted down by 5 bits during the translation
10197:             from CPU address to PCI address. The host address extension (IOC_HAE)
10198:             register extends the shifted address to 32 bits.
10199: 
10200:             The IOC_HAE register is accessed at address 18000000016. All bits of this
10201:             register are UNDEFINED after reset.
10202: 
10203:             Figure 6-2 shows the IOC_HAE register format, and Table 6-9 describes its
10204:             fields.
10205: 
10206: Figure 6-2 IOC_HAE Register Format
10207: 
10208: 63                        32 31 27 26              0
10209: 
10210:                      RES             HAE      RES
10211:                                     <31:27>
10212: 
10213: Table 6-9 IOC_HAE Register Field Description
10214: 
10215: Bits Field           Type Description
10216: 
10217: 63:32  RES           X    Reserved.
10218: 31:27
10219:        HAE<31:27> WO      Host address extension--This field is used for PCI
10220:                           address bits ad<31:27> during CPU-initiated PCI
10221: 26:0 RES             X    transactions when CPU physical address bits <31:29>
10222:                           are non-zero (see Table 6-1).
10223: 
10224:                           Reserved.
10225: 
10226: 6.4.2 Configuration Cycle Type Register
10227: 
10228:             During CPU-initiated PCI configuration space transfers, the configuration cycle
10229:             type (IOC_CONF) register supplies PCI address bits ad<1:0>.
10230: 
10231:             The IOC_CONF register is accessed at address 18000002016. All bits of this
10232:             register are UNDEFINED after reset.
10233: 
10234:             Figure 6-3 shows the IOC_CONF register format, and Table 6-10 describes its
10235:             fields.
10236: 
10237: 6-38 I/O Controller
10238: Figure 6-3 IOC_CONF Register Format                         210
10239: 
10240:  63                                                            CFG
10241:                                                                 AD
10242:                                                        RES    <1:0>
10243: 
10244: Table 6-10 IOC_CONF Register Field Description
10245: 
10246: Bits Field    Type Description
10247: 
10248: 63:2 RES      X   Reserved.
10249: 
10250: 1:0  CFG      WO  Configuration address-- This field is used as PCI
10251: 
10252:      AD<1:0>      address bits ad<1:0> during CPU-initiated PCI
10253: 
10254:                   configuration cycles.
10255: 
10256: 6.4.3 Status 0 Register
10257: 
10258:             The IOC logs error information in the status 0 (IOC_STAT0) register as follows:
10259: 
10260:             � If the ERR bit (<4>) is not set when an IOC error is detected, all of the
10261:                   register bits are updated and the ERR bit is set.
10262: 
10263:             � After the ERR bit is set, all of the register bits except the TREF, THIT, and
10264:                   LOST bits (<7:5>) are frozen until software clears the ERR bit.
10265: 
10266:             � If the ERR bit is set, and another IOC error is detected, the LOST bit is
10267:                   set. Because the ERR bit is set, information associated with the second
10268:                   error cannot be logged and is lost. The LOST bit remains set until it is
10269:                   cleared by software.
10270: 
10271:             The IOC_STAT0 register is accessed at address 18000004016. All bits of this
10272:             register are UNDEFINED after reset.
10273: 
10274:             Figure 6-4 shows the IOC_STAT0 register format, and Table 6-11 describes its
10275:             fields.
10276: 
10277:                                                             I/O Controller 6-39
10278: Figure 6-4 IOC_STAT0 Register Format
10279: 
10280: 63                         32 31                 13 12 11 10 8 7 6 5 4 3 0
10281: 
10282:                      RES                P_NBR    RES  CODE   T  T  L      E  CMD
10283:                                         <31:13>       <2:0>  R  H  O      R  <3:0>
10284:                                                              E  I  S      R
10285:                                                              F  T  T
10286: 
10287: Table 6-11 IOC_STAT0 Register Field Description
10288: 
10289: Bits Field           Type  Description
10290: 
10291: 63:32 RES            X     Reserved.
10292: 
10293: 31:13 P_NBR RO             Page number--This field stores starting address bits
10294:              <31:13>       ad<31:13> of a PCI-initiated transfer during which a
10295:                            page table read error or invalid page table error was
10296: 12:11 RES            X     detected.
10297: 
10298: 10:8 CODE            RO    Reserved.
10299: 
10300:     <2:0>                  Error code--This field contains the error code for the
10301:                            error condition that caused the ERR bit (<4>) to be
10302:                            set. This field is encoded as follows:
10303: 
10304:                            Code Error Condition
10305: 
10306:                            CPU-Initiated Transfers
10307: 
10308:                            000 Retry limit
10309:                            001 No device
10310:                            010 Bad data parity
10311:                            011 Target abort
10312: 
10313:                            PCI-Initiated Transfers
10314: 
10315:                            100 Bad address parity
10316:                            101 Page table read error
10317:                            110 Invalid page
10318:                            111 Data error
10319: 
10320: 7   TREF             RO    Test reference--This bit is for diagnostic testing. This
10321: 
10322:                            bit is set if the last PCI transaction referenced an
10323: 
10324:                            address in a target window. This bit is clear if the last
10325: 
10326:                            PCI transaction referenced an address outside of the
10327: 
10328:                            address ranges defined by the target windows.
10329: 
10330:                                                       (continued on next page)
10331: 
10332: 6-40 I/O Controller
10333: Table 6-11 (Cont.) IOC_STAT0 Register Field Description
10334: 
10335: Bits Field  Type   Description
10336: 
10337: 6    THIT   RO     Test hit--This bit is for diagnostic testing. This bit
10338:             R/W1C  is set if the last PCI transaction that referenced an
10339: 5    LOST          address in a target window also hit in the translation
10340:             R/W1C  lookaside buffer (TLB, Section 6.3.3). This bit is clear
10341: 4    ERR    RO     if the last PCI transaction that referenced an address
10342:                    in a target window missed in the TLB.
10343: 3:0  CMD
10344:                    This bit is set when an error occurs and either of the
10345:      <3:0>         following conditions applies:
10346: 
10347:                    � The ERR bit (<4>) was set before the error
10348:                          occurred.
10349: 
10350:                    � The CODE field (<2:0>) value is 5 or 6 and
10351:                          the ERR bit was set at the beginning of the
10352:                          transaction that caused the error.
10353: 
10354:                    When set, this bit indicates that error-logging
10355:                    information associated with the new error was lost.
10356:                    This bit remains set until it is written with a one.
10357: 
10358:                    Error--This bit is set when the IOC logs an error.
10359:                    This bit remains set until it is written with a one.
10360: 
10361:                    Command--When the IOC detects an error, this
10362:                    field stores the PCI command code of either of the
10363:                    following:
10364: 
10365:                    � A CPU-initiated PCI transaction during which the
10366:                          error occurred.
10367: 
10368:                    � A PCI-initiated transaction during which the
10369:                          error, other than a page table read error or invalid
10370:                          page table error, occurred (see bits <31:13>).
10371: 
10372: 6.4.4 Status 1 Register
10373: 
10374:             The status 1 (IOC_STAT1) register stores the starting address of PCI
10375:             transactions during which the IOC detected an error other than a page
10376:             table read error or invalid page table error.
10377: 
10378:             The IOC_STAT1 register is accessed at address 18000006016. All bits of this
10379:             register are UNDEFINED after reset.
10380: 
10381:                                                          I/O Controller 6-41
10382: Figure 6-5 shows the IOC_STAT1 register format, and Table 6-12 describes its
10383: fields.
10384: 
10385: Figure 6-5 IOC_STAT1 Register Format
10386: 
10387: 63                                   32 31                                      0
10388: 
10389:                      RES                         ADDR
10390:                                                  <31:0>
10391: 
10392: Table 6-12 IOC_STAT1 Register Field Description
10393: 
10394: Bits Field           Type Description
10395: 
10396: 63:32 RES            X    Reserved.
10397: 
10398: 31:0 ADDR            RO   Address--When the IOC detects an error, this field
10399: 
10400:     <31:0>                stores either of the following addresses (ad<31:0>):
10401: 
10402:                           � If the error occurred during a CPU-initiated dense-
10403:                                 space burst, this field contains the address of the
10404:                                 first longword transferred during the current bus
10405:                                 cycle.
10406: 
10407:                           � If the error is a PCI-initiated data error
10408:                                 (IOC_STAT0 bits <10:8> = 111), this field contains
10409:                                 the PCI address of the errant longword.
10410: 
10411: 6.4.5 Translation Buffer Invalidate All Register
10412: 
10413:             A write operation to the translation buffer invalidate all (IOC_TBIA) register
10414:             invalidates all entries in the scatter-gather map TLB at the start of the next
10415:             PCI-initiated transaction (Section 6.3.3).
10416: 
10417:             The IOC_TBIA register is accessed at address 18000008016. All bits of this
10418:             register are reserved and are ignored on write operations and UNDEFINED on
10419:             read operations.
10420: 
10421: 6-42 I/O Controller
10422: 6.4.6 Translation Buffer Enable Register
10423: 
10424:             The translation buffer enable (IOC_TB_ENA) register determines whether the
10425:             scatter-gather map TLB is enabled (Section 6.3.3).
10426: 
10427:             The IOC_TB_ENA register is accessed at address 1800000A0. All bits of this
10428:             register are UNDEFINED after reset.
10429: 
10430:             Figure 6-6 shows the IOC_TB_ENA register format, and Table 6-13 describes
10431:             its fields.
10432: 
10433: Figure 6-6 IOC_TB_ENA Register Format             876              0
10434: 
10435:  63                                               T
10436:                                                   E RES
10437:                                             RES   N
10438: 
10439: Table 6-13 IOC_TB_ENA Register Field Description
10440: 
10441: Bits Field  Type Description
10442: 
10443: 63:8 RES    X   Reserved.
10444: 
10445: 7    TEN    WO  TLB enable--When written with a zero, this bit disables
10446: 
10447:                 the scatter-gather map TLB. The TLB remains disabled
10448: 
10449:                 until this bit is written with a one. Disabling the TLB
10450: 
10451:                 does not disable scatter-gather map translations.
10452: 
10453: 6:0  RES    X   Reserved.
10454: 
10455: 6.4.7 PCI Soft Reset Register
10456: 
10457:             The PCI soft reset (IOC_SFT_RST) register allows software to assert and
10458:             deassert the PCI reset signal rst_l. The required time that the rst_l signal is
10459:             asserted is system dependent and must be timed by software.
10460: 
10461:             The IOC_SFT_RST register is accessed at address 1800000C0. The RST bit
10462:             (<6>) will be set after reset.
10463: 
10464:             Figure 6-7 shows the IOC_SFT_RST register format, and Table 6-14 describes
10465:             its fields.
10466: 
10467:                                                   I/O Controller 6-43
10468: Figure 6-7 IOC_SFT_RST Register Format                   765 0
10469: 
10470:  63                                                         R
10471:                                                             S RES
10472:                                                     RES     T
10473: 
10474: Table 6-14 IOC_SFT_RST Register Field Description
10475: 
10476: Bits Field           Type Description
10477: 
10478: 63:7 RES             X   Reserved.
10479: 
10480: 6    RST             WO  Reset--When this bit is written with a one, the IOC
10481: 
10482:                          asserts the PCI reset signal rst_l. The rst_l signal
10483: 
10484:                          remains asserted until this bit is written with a zero.
10485: 
10486:                          This bit is also set and the rst_l signal is asserted when
10487:                          the chip reset signal reset_in_l is asserted.
10488: 
10489: 5:0  RES             X   Reserved.
10490: 
10491: 6.4.8 PCI Parity Disable Register
10492: 
10493:             The PCI parity disable (IOC_PAR_DIS) register determines whether IOC
10494:             parity checking and associated error reporting and logging are enabled.
10495: 
10496:             The IOC_PAR_DIS register is accessed at address 1800000E0. The PAR bit
10497:             (<5>) is cleared after reset.
10498: 
10499:             Figure 6-8 shows the IOC_PAR_DIS register format, and Table 6-15 describes
10500:             its fields.
10501: 
10502: Figure 6-8 IOC_PAR_DIS Register Format                   654 0
10503: 
10504:  63                                                         P
10505:                                                             A RES
10506:                                                    RES      R
10507: 
10508: 6-44 I/O Controller
10509: Table 6-15 IOC_PAR_DIS Register Field Description
10510: 
10511: Bits Field  Type Description
10512: 
10513: 63:6 RES    X   Reserved.
10514: 
10515: 5    PAR    WO  Parity--When set, this bit disables IOC parity checking
10516: 
10517:                 (comparing calculated parity to the par signal) as well
10518: 
10519:                 as the reporting and logging of parity errors detected by
10520: 
10521:                 the parity check.
10522: 
10523:                 In CPU-initiated PCI write transactions, the PCI target
10524:                 device reports parity errors on the perr_l signal, and
10525:                 the IOC logs a bad data parity error (CODE = 2) in the
10526:                 IOC_STAT0 register (Section 6.4.3). This bit does not
10527:                 disable such reporting.
10528: 
10529:                 This bit is initialized to zero.
10530: 
10531: 4:0  RES    X   Reserved.
10532: 
10533: 6.4.9 Window Base Registers
10534: 
10535:             There is one window base (IOC_W_BASE) register for each PCI target
10536:             window. These registers (IOC_W_BASE1, IOC_W_BASE0) specify the starting
10537:             addresses of the PCI target windows in PCI memory address space. The
10538:             window base addresses must be on a naturally aligned address boundary
10539:             equal to the window's size. Additionally, to ensure that only one PCI target
10540:             window can match an incoming PCI address, the PCI target windows must be
10768: 
10769:                           contains an interrupt vector supplied by external logic
10770: 
10771:                           and is returned to the CPU. When the IOC_IACK_SC
10772: 
10773:                           register is written, this field contains the data for a PCI
10774: 
10775:                           special cycle.
10776: 
10777: 6.4.14 Initialization Requirements
10778: 
10779:             After the system power is turned on or after a reset condition (reset_in_l is
10780:             asserted), serial ROM (SROM) code must initialize the IOC registers.
10781: 
10782:             The SROM code does the following:
10783: 
10784:             1. Deasserts the rst_l signal by writing zero (0) to the IOC_SFT_RST register
10785:                   (Section 6.4.7).
10786: 
10787: 6-50 I/O Controller
10788:             2. Clears the ERR and LOST bits by writing ones (1's) to IOC_STAT0 register
10789:                   bits <5:4> (Section 6.4.3).
10790: 
10791:             3. Prevents the IOC from responding to itself by writing zero to the
10792:                   IOC_W_BASE1 and IOC_W_BASE0 registers (Section 6.4.9).
10793: 
10794:             4. Initializes the HAE<31:27> field in the IOC_HAE register (Section 6.4.1).
10795: 
10796:             This level of initialization is sufficient to allow CPU-initiated PCI transfers to
10797:             operate correctly. Boot code must do additional initialization to configure the
10798:             IOC such that it can respond to PCI-initiated transfers (DMA).
10799: 
10800: 6.5 I/O Controller Signal Pins
10801: 
10802:             Sections 6.5.1 through 6.5.16 describe the IOC signal pins.
10803: 
10804: 6.5.1 req_l
10805: 
10806:             The req_l signal is asserted when the CPU needs to initiate a PCI transfer.
10807:             External arbitration logic is required.
10808: 
10809:             The req_l signal is an output-only signal and can be tristated.
10810: 
10811: 6.5.2 gnt_l
10812: 
10813:             The gnt_l signal is asserted by external arbitration logic when the CPU is
10814:             granted ownership of the PCI bus. Digital recommends that the arbiter default
10815:             grant (park) the PCI bus to the CPU when no other device is requesting
10816:             ownership.
10817: 
10818:             During configuration cycles (read or write operations), the IOC performs
10819:             address stepping (drives address and command for two PCI cycles). If the
10820:             gnt_l signal is deasserted after the IOC has driven the address and command
10821:             for one cycle but before the frame_l signal is asserted, the IOC will relinquish
10822:             the bus and wait for the gnt_l signal to be asserted and the PCI bus idle
10823:             condition.
10824: 
10825:                                                                  Note
10900: 
10901: 6.5.10 stop_l
10902: 
10903:             The target of a PCI transaction drives the stop_l signal to request that the
10904:             initiator stop the current transaction.
10905:             The stop_l signal is a bidirectional signal and can be tristated.
10906: 
10907: 6.5.11 par
10908: 
10909:             The par signal is the even-parity signal for the ad<31:0> and c_be_l<3:0>
10910:             signals.
10911:             The par signal is a bidirectional signal and can be tristated.
10912: 
10913: 6.5.12 perr_l
10914: 
10915:             The perr_l signal is asserted when a data parity error has been detected.
10916:             The perr_l signal is a bidirectional signal and can be tristated.
10917: 
10918: 6.5.13 rst_l
10919: 
10920:             The rst_l signal is the PCI reset signal. It is generated by the CPU. The RST
10921:             bit (<6>) in the IOC_SFT_RST register (Section 6.4.7) allows the rst_l signal to
10922:             be asserted under software control. The rst_l signal is automatically asserted
10923:             when the reset_in_l signal is asserted.
10924:             The rst_l signal is an output-only signal.
10925: 
10926:                                                                                                                I/O Controller 6-53
10927: 6.5.14 pci_clk_in
10928: 
10929:             The pci_clk_in signal provides timing for all transactions on the PCI bus.
10930:             All of the IOC PCI signals, except the rst_l signal, are synchronous with the
10931:             pci_clk_in signal. Inputs are sampled on the rising edge of the pci_clk_in
10932:             signal; outputs change state as a result of the rising edge of the pci_clk_in
10933:             signal.
10934:             The pci_clk_in signal is an input-only signal.
10935: 
10936: 6.5.15 memreq_l
10937: 
10938:             The memreq_l signal is a PCI sideband, input-only signal and is synchronous
10939:             with the pci_clk_in signal. The IOC arbitrates (internal to the chip) for access
10940:             to memory when it samples the memreq_l signal asserted. The IOC asserts
10941:             the memack_l signal when it wins arbitration for memory access.
10942: 
12638: VGR      Video and graphics   1 2000 0080    three cycles). All other bits are
12639:          control                             UNDEFINED.
12640: PLM      Plane mask           1 2000 0088    UNDEFINED
12641:          Foreground           1 2000 0090
12642: FPOMRR3  Power management     1 2000 0098    UNDEFINED
12643:                                              UNDEFINED
12644:                                              DMA_OVR = 0, INT_OVR = 0,
12645:                                              OVR_DIV = 0, PRM_DIV = 0, all other
