 9480: 
 9481: 6.2.18 PCI Master Abort
 9482: 
 9483:             The IOC terminates a CPU-initiated PCI transaction if no PCI device responds
 9484:             by asserting devsel_l within five PCI clock cycles after the IOC asserts the
 9485:             frame_l signal. The IOC will not retry this transfer on the PCI. If the CPU-
 9486:             initiated access was a read operation, the IOC returns arbitrary data with
 9487:             error status to the CPU, which causes an instruction exception. The IOC also
 9488:             reports the error to the CPU with an internal interrupt, and logs the error
 9489:             condition.
 9490: 
 9491:                                                                                                                I/O Controller 6-21
 9492:             The IOC does the following:
 9494:             � Sets the ERR bit (if it is not set) in the IOC status 0 register (IOC_STAT0,
 9495:                   Section 6.4.3).
 9496: 
 9497:             � Sets the error CODE field to 1 (no device) in the IOC_STAT0 register,
 9498:                   except for CPU-initiated PCI special cycles.
 9499: 
 9500:             � Logs the PCI command code of the CPU-initiated transfer in which the
 9501:                   error was detected into the CMD field of the IOC_STAT0 register. The
 9502:                   command code indicates the type of PCI cycle in which the error occurred.
 9503: 
 9504:             � Logs the starting address of the CPU-initiated PCI transfer into the ADDR
 9505:                   field of the IOC status 1 register (IOC_STAT1, Section 6.4.4).
 9506: 
 9507:             The ERR bit and the logged error information are frozen until the ERR bit is
 9508:             cleared by writing a one to it.
 9509: 
 9510:             If the ERR bit was set, the IOC sets the LOST bit in the IOC_STAT0 register.
 9511:             The LOST bit remains set until it is cleared by writing a one to it. The CODE
 9512:             and CMD error information is logged only for the error that caused the ERR
 9513:             bit to be set--such information for the second error is lost.
 9514: 
 9515: 6.3 Peripheral-Initiated PCI Cycles
 9516: 
 9517:             The IOC is a PCI target when it responds to cycles initiated by a PCI
 9518:             peripheral device or external DMA controller operating as a bus master. The
 9519:             IOC treats PCI-initiated masked read operations as unmasked read operations.
 9520:             Arbitrary byte-enable combinations for PCI-initiated write transfers are
 9521:             implemented by read-modify-write operations in the memory controller. PCI-
 9522:             initiated transfers can target pages of memory that are mapped in cacheable
 9523:             or noncacheable address spaces. The chip ensures that memory coherency is
 9524:             maintained for all PCI-initiated transfers to cacheable memory.
 9525: 
 9526:             PCI devices cannot access registers that are internal to the chip.
 9527: 
 9528: 6.3.1 PCI Cycle Command Code Support and Aliasing
 8605:             the CPU by an internal interrupt. External logic can report the assertion of
 8606:             the serr_l signal by PCI peripherals using the chip's interrupt signal pins
 8607: 
 8608: 6-2 I/O Controller
 8609:             irq<2:0>. The serr_l signal is an asynchronous PCI signal that is usually
 8610:             asserted one cycle after valid parity is driven on the par signal (that is, two
 8611:             cycles after valid address or data). PCI peripherals can also use the serr_l
 8612:             signal to report other severe error conditions.
 8613: 
 8614:             Data parity errors on all PCI transactions (except during special cycles) are
 8615:             reported with the PCI perr_l signal. Only data parity errors are reported on
 8616:             the perr_l line. The chip provides a signal pin to connect the perr_l signal
 8617:             directly to the IOC. The perr_l signal is synchronous with the PCI clock. Data
 8618:             parity errors are reported on the perr_l signal one cycle after valid parity is
 8619:             driven on the par signal (that is, two cycles after valid data).
 8620: 
 8621:             The IOC parity disable register (IOC_PAR_DIS, Section 6.4.8) allows parity
 8622:             checking and reporting to be disabled. When this register is written, the IOC
 8623:             does not log or report parity errors.
 8624: 
 8625: 6.1.4 PCI Exclusive Access Support
 8626: 
 8627:             The IOC supports the PCI exclusive access protocol for peripheral-initiated
 8628:             transfers with system memory (also called direct memory access or DMA). The
 8629:             chip cannot generate exclusive access cycles for CPU-initiated PCI transfers.
 8630: 
 8631: 6.1.5 Clocking
 8632: 
 8633:             With the exception of the PCI reset signal rst_l, all PCI signals are
 9338:             even parity on the ad<31:0> and c_be_l<3:0> signals. The calculated parity is
 9339:             compared to the parity received on the par pin in the PCI clock cycle following
 9340:             a valid data transfer (the irdy_l and trdy_l signals are asserted). A read data
 9341:             parity error is detected when the comparison fails. In such cases, the IOC
 9342:             reports the error to the CPU with an internal interrupt, and logs the error
 9343:             condition as described below. Additionally, the read data returned to the CPU
 9344:             is flagged with an error, which causes an instruction exception.
 9345: 
 9346: 6-18 I/O Controller
 9347:             When bad data parity is detected, the IOC does the following:
 9348: 
 9349:             � Sets the ERR bit (if it is not set) in the IOC status 0 register (IOC_STAT0,
 9350:                   Section 6.4.3).
 9351: 
 9352:             � Sets the error CODE field to 2 (bad data parity) in the IOC_STAT0 register.
 9353: 
 9354:             � Logs the PCI command code of the CPU-initiated transfer in which the
 9355:                   error was detected into the CMD field of the IOC_STAT0 register. The
 9356:                   command code indicates that the error occurred during a PCI read cycle.
 9357: 
 9358:             � Logs the starting address of the CPU-initiated PCI transfer into the ADDR
 9359:                   field of the IOC status 1 register (IOC_STAT1, Section 6.4.4).
 9360: 
 9361:             The ERR bit and the logged error information are frozen until the ERR bit is
 9362:             cleared by writing a one to it.
 9363: 
 9364:             If the ERR bit was set, the IOC sets the LOST bit in the IOC_STAT0 register.
 9365:             The LOST bit remains set until it is cleared by writing a one to it. The CODE
 9366:             and CMD error information is logged only for the error that caused the ERR
 9367:             bit to be set--such information for the second error is lost.
 9368: 
 9369: 6.2.13.2 PCI Write Data Parity Errors
 9370:             During a CPU-initiated PCI write data transfer, the IOC drives and calculates
 9371:             even parity on the ad<31:0> and c_be_l<3:0> signals. The IOC drives the
 9372:             calculated parity on the par pin in the PCI clock cycle after it drives the
 9373:             write data. PCI devices that support parity checking calculate even parity
 9374:             on received ad<31:0> and c_be_l<3:0> signals when they are the target of
 9375:             PCI memory, I/O, or configuration write cycles. The target samples the par
 9376:             signal one cycle after a valid data transfer (the irdy_l and trdy_l signals are
 9377:             asserted). A write data parity error is detected when the calculated parity and
 9378:             sampled parity comparison fails. In such cases, the target asserts perr_l in the
 9379:             next cycle (two cycles after a valid data transfer). The IOC reports the error to
 9380:             the CPU with an internal interrupt, and logs the error condition as described
 9381:             in Section 6.2.13.1; however, in this case, the CMD field in the IOC_STAT0
 9382:             register indicates that the error was generated during a PCI write cycle.
 9383: 
 9384: 6.2.14 PCI Master Timeout
 9385: 
 9386:             The PCI Local Bus Specification, Revision 2.0 specifies a mechanism to limit
 9387:             the duration of a bus master's burst sequence. The mechanism requires PCI
 9388:             masters to implement a latency timer that counts the number of cycles since
 9389:             the assertion of the frame_l signal. If the master's latency timer expires and
 9390:             the master's gnt_l signal has been deasserted, the master must surrender
 9391:             the bus. This mechanism prevents masters from holding bus ownership for
 9392:             extended periods of time, but limits throughput as well as latency.
 9393: 
 1500:                   - Three level-sensitive hardware interrupts are sourced by pins irq<2:0>.
 1501: 
 1502:                   - Two internally generated interrupts respond to external interface error
 1503:                        conditions. These are sourced by the memory controller error status
 1504:                        register (ESR, Section 5.6.6) and the I/O controller status register 0
 1505:                        (IOC_STAT0, Section 6.4.3).
 1506: 
 1507:             � Software interrupts
 1508:                   The 15 prioritized software interrupts are sourced by the software interrupt
 1509:                   request register (SIRR, Section 4.1.13.2).
 1510: 
 1511:             � Asynchronous system traps (ASTs)
 1512:                   Four ASTs are available, one for each processor mode: user, supervisor,
 1513:                   executive, and kernel. These traps are sourced by the asynchronous trap
 1514:                   request register (ASTRR, Section 4.1.13.3).
 1515: 
 1516:             In addition to the three level-sensitive hardware interrupts from the irq<2:0>
 1517:             pins, error conditions detected in the memory controller and the IOC can
 1518:             interrupt the CPU.
 1519: 
 1520:                                                                                                        Internal Architecture 2-5
 1521:             The memory controller generates an interrupt if it detects any error condition,
 1522:             such as a nonexistent memory access, a missed refresh cycle, an uncorrectable
 1523:             ECC error, and a Bcache parity error. To return the memory subsystem to
 1524:             correct operation, software must clear these error conditions by writing to the
 1525:             memory controller ESR.
 1526: 
 1527:             The I/O controller generates an interrupt to the CPU when it detects certain
 1528:             error conditions. These errors are logged in the IOC_STAT0 register. To
 1529:             remove the interrupt, software must write a one to the error bit in the
 1530:             IOC_STAT0 register.
 1531: 
 1532:             The memory controller and IOC error conditions and registers are described in
 1533:             Chapters 5 and 6.
 1534: 
 1535:             All interrupts can be independently masked using onchip enable registers.
 1536:             In addition, AST interrupts are qualified by the current processor mode
 1537:             and the current state of software interrupt enable register bit 2 (SIER,
 1538:             Section 4.1.13.5). Providing distinct enable bits for each independent interrupt

codex
The manual contradicts two key premises: STAT1 is read-only, and only ERR/LOST in STAT0 are W1C. SFT_RST is a write-only, latched reset level that software must release; PAR_DIS is also write-only. A store-and-echo test would therefore assert behavior the manual does not promise.
